   "en": {
    "headline": "Matthew Honnibal calls the GPT-6 Astra study misleading; Simon Willison replicates part of it with Qwen 3.8 27B",
    "summary_short": "The study crediting GPT-6 Astra with reading and editing weights mixes different techniques, according to Matthew Honnibal. Simon Willison repeated one of its tests with an open model on his laptop.",
    "summary_long": "Matthew Honnibal, author of spaCy, reviewed the study's experiments. Only three truly work \"by looking at the weights\", without tools, and on small networks of 17,000 to 172,000 weights. The headline result, which lifts Qwen3.5-0.8B from 7.9% to 48.1% on MATH-500, used Python programs and ran the model up to 50 times. For Honnibal, that is what a human engineer would do, and it shouldn't be presented alongside direct weight reading.\n\nSimon Willison focused on the no-tools part: guessing which game a network was trained on just from its weights. Qwen 3.8 27B, running on his laptop, got Pac-Man, Pong and Breakout right, after 46 to 65 minutes of reasoning per test, and failed on Freeway. Willison finds it impressive, but thinks a person with pencil and paper could do it, and that much of the commentary around the study is overblown.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
