   "en": {
    "headline": "A user reports that Postman installed plugins in Codex and Claude Code without permission",
    "summary_short": "According to a GitHub report, the Postman desktop app installed plugins in Codex and Claude Code without asking. It also tried to approve, on its own, an instruction that loads at the start of every session.",
    "summary_long": "A Mac user posted a report with logs and source code in Postman's repository. According to them, when the desktop app was opened it installed a plugin in Codex and another in Claude Code, a remote MCP connection and the Postman CLI. The installer also tried to mark its own hook as trusted: an instruction that runs when the agent starts and tells it to use Postman for any API task. The author clarifies that no data theft has been shown.\n\nA reply in the thread on behalf of Postman explains that the plugins are bundled to make setup easier, that this is mentioned on the download page and that they can be uninstalled. It promises to make the opt-in clearer and to review the hooks. Many users compare it to malware, and another claims the notice was added to the website afterwards.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": [
     "Postman desktop 12.33.4 automatically installed plugins in Codex and Claude Code and the Postman CLI without explicit consent.",
     "The installer attempts to approve its own startup hook in Codex.",
     "Postman added the notice to its download page after the issue was reported."
    ]
   },
